---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Thomas Dimnet"
  description="I build systems, and I teach engineers to build them. The hard part is the same in both: deciding what to leave out. Lead Educational Engineer at Scaleway, founder of NX Academy."
  caret={true}
>
  <div class="region">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="col">
        <h1>
          I build systems, and I teach engineers to build them. The hard part is
          the same in both: deciding what to leave out.
        </h1>
        <p class="proof">
          Thirteen years of building software and cloud systems, and just as
          long teaching them. Today at Scaleway, I train the engineers who train
          everyone else.
        </p>
        <dl class="now">
          <div class="row">
            <dt>Now</dt>
            <dd>
              Lead Educational Engineer, Software &amp; Cloud — <strong
                >Scaleway</strong
              >, Paris
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="row">
            <dt>Also</dt>
            <dd>
              Founder of <a href="https://nx.academy/">NX Academy</a>, a free
              school for developers, in French ↗
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="mvt-kicker">The build side</p>
        <h2>The choice I'm proudest of doesn't look like much.</h2>
        <p>
          As CTO of SensCritique, I had to containerize our infrastructure. The
          expected answer was Kubernetes — the powerful one, the one that looks
          good on a CV. I went with Docker Swarm instead: smaller, plainer, and
          something the whole team could run and repair on a Sunday night
          without me.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">I could have picked Kubernetes for the photo.</p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          I'd make the same call today, and I make it every time I design a
          course: the content is never the hard part, deciding what to leave out
          is.
        </p>
        <p class="meta"><a href="/work">How that rebuild went →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="mvt-kicker">The teaching side</p>
        <h2>The course where I couldn't cut anything.</h2>
        <p>
          I wrote a course on design patterns in JavaScript. Nine patterns, the
          full catalog — the exact opposite of leaving things out. So where's
          the refusal?
        </p>
        <p>
          Not in what I removed; a course on patterns has to cover the patterns.
          It's in the order. You don't open with a pattern. You open with the
          objects and inheritance that make patterns readable at all. Then they
          arrive, one at a time, each one starting from a real problem rather
          than a definition.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">
        The inventory is real. What I refuse is to dump it all at once.
      </p>
      <div class="col">
        <p class="meta">
          <a href="https://nx.academy/">The course is public, in French →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav class="route" aria-label="Sections">
      <p class="route-kicker">Read on</p>
      <p class="line">
        <strong>Method</strong> — how I design courses and train the trainers, with
        the work that proves it. <a href="/method">→</a>
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        <strong>Work</strong> — the systems I've built, and the games I'm building
        now. <a href="/work">→</a>
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        <strong>Speaking</strong> — what I talk about, and how to get me in the room.
        <a href="/speaking">→</a>
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        <strong>About</strong> — the long version of how I ended up doing both.
        <a href="/about">→</a>
      </p>
    </nav>

    <section class="close">
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          I'm always up for a conversation about teaching engineers — a training
          program to build, a talk to give, or just the problem itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 96px 0 8px;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 4.7vw, 52px);
    line-height: 1.14;
    margin: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    max-width: 30rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .hero .proof {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 34px 0 0;
    max-width: 33rem;
  }
  .now {
    margin-top: 34px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    max-width: 33rem;
  }
  .now .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 64px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .now dt {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--meta);
    padding-top: 3px;
  }
  .now dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .now dd strong {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .mvt-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--meta);
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .route {
    margin-top: 96px;
    padding-top: 40px;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .route-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--meta);
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }
  .route .line {
    padding: 20px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .route .line:first-of-type {
    border-top: 0;
  }
  .route .line strong {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 19px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Hero entrance: title "compiles" left→right, then proof/now rise-fade in. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .hero h1 {
      clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
      animation: wipeIn 1.25s 0.15s cubic-bezier(0.33, 0.1, 0.25, 1) forwards;
    }
    .hero .proof {
      opacity: 0;
      animation: riseIn 0.6s 0.95s ease forwards;
    }
    .hero .now {
      opacity: 0;
      animation: riseIn 0.6s 1.15s ease forwards;
    }
  }
</style>
